/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A bad system is one where only the incumbents are able to benefit. In such a system, innovation is reduced, and the total surplus is usually reduced. Systems with entrenched power hierarchies are usually ones that benefit only the incumbents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for both political and economic systems, as in such system, the consequences of only incumbents benefiting is large.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hungary&#x27;s economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption\">Hungary's economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption</a><ul><li>This is an example of a system that is currently rather corrupt and benefits only incumbents.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>This highlights how without certain economic freedoms (e.g., economic mobility), political freedom is likewise impossible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>Highlights a trait of a good institution, the exact opposite of what is described herein.</li></ul></li></ul>","American moralism sets it most in line with a liberal theory of international relations":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is some fundamental sense in which American moralism, i.e., the belief that virtue should be institutionalized, that evil people should be destroyed, lends itself well with the liberal theory of international relations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the reason that realist ideas of international relations are quite unpopular in the US.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that American’s defining itself as the “city upon a hill” idea/ideas of American exceptionalism means that Americans have an unique aversion to the idea of acting “without morals” and using underhanded tactics.</p><h4>References</h4>","Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Chinese philosophy, unless the philosophy of the West, is not concerned with increasing positive knowledge, but rather with searching for super-moral values, an understanding of the world that helps one practically deal with worldly travails and achieve the status of the sage.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This identifies something that structurally separates Western and Eastern philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The end goal of Chinese philosophy, as identified by Fong Youlan, is “sageliness within and kingliness without.” In this way, the enlightened individual is not merely a citizen of some particular polity, but also a “citizen of the universe (天民).”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>It seems that perhaps part of the reason for the development of philosophy in this direction is that this way, philosophy would pose less of a threat to the central power of the state, since it far more focused on understanding humans, which is controllable by the state, whereas an upturning of the metaphysical nature of the world would undermine state power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>It seems that a similar attitude pervaded Chinese science.</li></ul></li></ul>","Determinism and free will are incompatible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that determinism and free will are incompatible (i.e., they cannot both be true at the same time). Does not say whether determinism or free will is correct.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the first step to proving either hard determinism, or libertarianism.</p><h4>Arguments</h4><p><strong>The Consequence Argument</strong></p><ul><li>If determinism is true, then our actions are the necessary consequence of the laws of nature and events in the distant past.</li><li>You cannot change the past, nor can you change the laws of nature.</li><li>Therefore, you cannot change the consequences of them (your actions).</li><li>Therefore, compatibilism is false.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, it leads to competitors attempting to create something that is similar, leading to much duplicate research being conducted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important as this flaw of the patent system is not often recognized.</p><h4>Caveat</h4><p>At the same time, if there was no patent system, it seems that people would lack incentive to conduct research anyways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li></ul>","Encompassing interests mean that an actor's private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor's gains most from rent-seeking":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An encompassing interest is one such that an actor’s private stake is intertwined with the stake of the broader society. This means that the actor will undertake actions that not only benefits itself, but also the entire society in which it exists. A narrow interest is one such that a actor’s private stake is disconnected and opposite the interests of the broader society. This means that it will engage in rent-seeking behavior.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This allows for the categorization of incentives along a spectrum of encompassing to narrow, allowing for the analysis of policy implications along those lines.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A government has an encompassing interest in ensuring stable economic growth, since with economic growth its own tax base expands. Therefore, it will work to ensure that the economy grows. On the other hand, oil companies could be considered a narrow interest. It engages in business activities that harm the environment, which is detrimental to the society around it, although the company itself profits from this damage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation\">A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide\">Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide</a></li></ul>","Environment variables are global settings that programs can read at runtime":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Environment variables are global settings that programs are able to read when they are run. For example, <code>PATH</code> gives the directories to find executable files.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Environment variables allow for programs to be run on different computers with little changes required (as they are built into all operating systems).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics\">The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics</a><ul><li>Paper money is analogous to a “global setting,” as it standardizes units of value and makes exchange easier.</li></ul></li></ul>","Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the increased risks of powerful generals rising up in an attempt to take the emperorship, emperors began centralizing control of the army so that the military moved where the emperor did.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final evolution of Roman border defense as troops were removed from the border and centralized around the emperor in order to ensure that power was centralized. This made border defense difficult, since it required long distance travel in order to reach a particular spot and only one border defense issue can be resolve at one time.</p><h4>References</h4>","In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In Receptor Tyrosine Kinases, signals are amplified through kinase cascades, which the receptor itself triggers (by phosphorylating a kinase itself). In G-Protein-Coupled Receptors, an enzyme like adenylyl cyclase creates many cAMP (second messenger), which amplifies the signal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two different signal transduction pathways have different means of amplifying the signal, which is something that I often forget.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification\">In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification</a></li></ul>","Interoperability in US software benefits US AI development":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>American interoperability is an asset for US AI development, since it allows for better training of agents that make them more capable in the field.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a persistent structural gap between AI development in the US compared to China.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Interoperability allows the RL process to meet with greater friction and in a way that allows the model to train against real-world surfaces, which strengthens the model.</p><ul><li>This is part of the reason for China’s models being behind the US, since China’s ecosystem is much more closed than the US, and it is dominated by a few central players that are in control of everything, so there is no interoperability, because there is no need for interoperability.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>But this also gives Chinese AI an advantage in that since they are so integrated, they get device distribution, not just model distribution. Device distribution provides a greater data loop that selling software alone does not provide.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities\">There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities</a><ul><li>This is a reason for this being the case.</li></ul></li></ul>","Loser's consent is a crucial aspect of democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Loser’s consent is the idea that the losers in an election will not deny the results of that election. This is crucial for an democracy, as it ensures that the losers of any election will not simply deny the results and prevent a peaceful transfer of power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant aspects for a stable democratic system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three key factors that underlie loser’s consent:</p><ol><li>Belief in a common nation: without this, there is no incentive for the defeated party to not attempt to hold on to power.</li><li>Belief in a fair electoral system: without this, there is no reason for the defeated party to accept the results of the election.</li><li>Belief in the existence of future elections: without this, defeated parties would remain defeated, which would incentive a coercive hold on power now.</li></ol><p>Many are arguing that today, American democracy is failing to meet the three standards set about here, and thus, that American democracy is backsliding.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li></ul>","Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many are arguing that 2006 was the apex of the current wave of democratization, and that democratization has be in a downward decline following that point (around the same time as the Great Recession).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a backsliding of democracy again, as has happened in the previous waves, although this time, Democratic Backsliding has become more insidious, as authoritarians are allowing Competitive Authoritarianism and the slow corruption of democratic values, rather than more direct means, such as a violent coup.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One way in which this manifests is in the rise of competitive authoritarianism, whereby authoritarian strong-men vie for political power within an ostensibly democratic system though when they gain power, they begin stripping away at the democratic substructure of the state. These figures, somewhat controversially, include:</p><ul><li>Vladimir Putin,</li><li>Victor Orban (now removed from power),</li><li>Nicholas Maduro.</li><li>Alexander Lushenko.</li><li>Donald Trump</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>People might be confusing the two terms here. It seems more accurate to say that constitutional liberalism is what is being threatened, not “democracy.”</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism undermines democracy\">Capitalism undermines democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li></ul>","Money is a psychological construct":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Money is a psychological construct since the currency itself has no intrinsic value and its use is merely something to make transactions easier.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument from Harari’s <em>Homo Sapiens</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the value of the money itself is nil, the entire value of a piece of money comes from the psychological beliefs associated with the money. This, Harari argues, was a part of the Cognitive Revolution that he hypothesizes.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems to me however that many of the currencies of the ancient past did have value, such as gold, since they were in great demand and they were scarce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities\">Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities</a><ul><li>This is essentially my critique.</li></ul></li></ul>","Morality is relative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is no objective set of moral truths in the world. Rather, different people or groups create their own versions of morality which are all equally valid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a viewpoint that had been held by many intellectuals throughout time.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>The Rationalist School of Neo-Taoism</h5><p>Guo Xiang argues that since there is disagreement regarding the truth of moral claims, that there must then not be objective morality.</p><h5>The Sentimentalist School of Neo-Taoism</h5><p>Simply claims that one should live as they want to, without regard for the customs and morals of one’s time. This does not mean descending into sensual pleasure, however. It merely means living according to one’s impulse, which is often highly refined. This is expressed in the concept of <em>feng liu</em>. Most of the time, people of this schools were sentimental regarding nature or the cosmos, rather than their personal tragedies or successes.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Guo Xiang’s argument is clearly fallacious, since such an argument can easily be applied to the very debate at hand, and thereby refute Guo’s position.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a><ul><li>This note opposes the linked note, at least for ethical truths.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the Range Property Argument. People are not apportioned a greater share of justice based on their capacity for justice beyond a certain limit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Explains the way that the principles of justice are to be applied.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Once someone meets a certain limit in their capacity for justice, they are no longer awarded further considerations of justice corresponding to above that limit. Of course, it is difficult to pin down the exactly threshold above which one is no longer entitled to greater justice.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>An obvious objection is that it seems arbitrary that people who pass beyond a specific threshold should not receive more justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>The reason for the thing in this note is to ensure this.</li></ul></li></ul>","Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A more precise statement is that social and economic inequalities are to be arranged so that they are both reasonably expected to be for the advantage of all, and attached to positions and offices open to all.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the second aspect of Rawls’ conception of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>The first principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","Some cell organelles are related to bacteria":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are some cell organelles which originated as bacteria cells in a luminal space. For example, the mitochondria.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains many odd features of these organelles, including how they have two cell membranes, have some chromosomes, and have internal ribosomes different from those extant in the cytoplasm.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some cell organelles are related to bacteria\">Some cell organelles are related to bacteria</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes\">Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes</a></li></ul>","The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A friendship that is based on mutual virtue is both long lasting and fruitful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle considers this form of friendship to the best of all of the types of friendships.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friendships based on mutual virtue are perfect.</p><ul><li>They last for a long time, since virtue is a disposition and dispositions are not easily changed.</li><li>They take time to develop, since two people need to prove to each other that they are deserving of love.<ul><li>A person ought to be loved by another both universally (generally, absolutely, based on general goodness/pleasantness) and contingently (good/pleasant for themself).</li></ul></li><li>They encompass both the friendship of utility and of pleasure, since good people are both useful and pleasant to other good people.</li><li>This is the only type of friendship where there is true selflessness, where you wish good for the friend for the friend’s sake, not for personal gain.<ul><li>This connects back to the principle Aristotle states, which is that it is more important to love than to be loved in a friendship.</li><li>When one wishes well for a friend, it is out of a moral state (disposition), not just a passing feeling.</li></ul></li><li>These types of friendships are rare.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a><ul><li>Friendships of virtue can be contrasted with friendships of utility or pleasure.</li></ul></li></ul>","The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that the rational soul is composed of two parts: the scientific (contemplative) and calculative (deliberative).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two different parts of the soul serve different purposes when it comes to intellectual virtues.</p><h4>Scientific vs. Calculative</h4><ul><li><strong>Scientific:</strong> the study of “necessary/eternal truths,” i.e., mathematics, first principles, etc.</li><li><strong>Calculative:</strong> the study of “contingent/variable truths,” i.e., what to do in a particular circumstance, how to act in a particular circumstance, etc.</li></ul><p>It is the goal of both parts of this rational soul to search for truth. What each part of the rational soul does well is their virtue, and these virtues are what allows each part of the soul to search for the truth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a><ul><li>The complementary part of the soul.</li></ul></li></ul>","Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it is often assumed that either the heretics or the orthodoxy is right, a far more common occurrence is that both are right to some degree, and that a synthesis of the two viewpoints produces the best approximation of the underlying truth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains another situation in which it is beneficial to allow for freedom of expression.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>This note explains another possibility beyond the two covered in the linked note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the future, trade in increasingly be influence by the culture of the nations engaged in trade.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that specific trade blocs may emerge that are culturally specific.</p><h4>Japan</h4><p>Because Japan is its own civilization, this means that they may have difficulty trading with others in the future.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>This note is a refutation of the idea presented in the linked note here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that virtue must be repeatedly practiced in order for it to be manifest in a person, that an action is not merely virtuous if it is the result of some fleeting impulse and not fundamentally a part of someone’s character.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s most important point regarding the nature of virtue, that it must be constantly practiced.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This take take many forms, even beyond what would normally be considered standard moral education. For instance,  music is something Aristotle believed that had a great impact on our souls and our dispositions and thus our ability to act virtuously. Thomas Jefferson seems to have been influenced similarly.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“<a href=\"https://www.liberalism.org/p/jefferson-s-liberal-morals?curius=7539&amp;trail=07a868ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jefferson’s Liberal Morals</a>”</p><p>As vital as Jefferson found traditional classroom education, he thought that the government could help cultivate the moral sense in one other important respect: through funding fine art. This may strike the modern reader as unusual or frivolous, but Jefferson frequently argued that <strong>exposure to beautiful things could elevate the human soul.</strong> More than most American politicians today, he believed that the visual world that surrounds us plays an important role in shaping who we are.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>When it talks about “\\[acting] in a virtuous state” here, it is specifically referring to acting consistently, over a fixed period of time, in a virtuous state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The condition for a happy life is sustained virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Washington’s Farewell Address outline a foreign policy stance that was strictly isolationist. He argued against the US getting embroiled in future conflicts in distant places and instead focus more directly on the American continent/domestic issues.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>In a broad sense, this imperative held until the US intervention in the First World War, meaning that it significantly shaped the US view of foreign policy in the periods leading up to that point.</p><h4>References</h4>","Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that Western government is better envisioned as being built upon Constitutional Liberalism, and not Democracy (mass plebiscite).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers an unique view on the nature of Western governments, deriving from the distinction between Democracy and Constitutional Liberalism.</p><h4>Source</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Fareed Zakaria</p><p>The “Western model of government” is best symbolized not by the mass plebiscite but the impartial judge.</p></blockquote><h4>Evidence</h4><ul><li>Ever since Roman times, there has been a strong traditional in the Western world of preserving certain individual rights and enforcing the rule of law.</li><li>\"Democracy\" in the sense of a mass plebiscite did not really originate in any meaningful capacity until the 19th century, in the US and the UK.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy and Constitutional Liberalism</a></li></ul>","While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Most people share the belief that</p><ul><li>There should be no unfounded/irrational distinctions between people in assigning rights and duties.</li><li>There should be a proper/fair distribution of social advantages.</li></ul><p>However, they would disagree as to how those principles are arrived upon.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls is making clear the distinction between the concept of justice and conceptions of justice. In the rest of the book, it is explaining his conception of Justice and why is the best one.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This is meant to be extremely deflationary, since Rawls will fill in the structure step up here with his own conception of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Aristotle's take on the object of justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a><ul><li>Aristotle distinguishes distributive justice as a particular form of justice, offering his conception of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
